PreviewSession (internal/engine/preview.go) keeps a single torrent.Client + torrent.Torrent alive for the whole interactive add-flow preview -- both resolving metadata and serving live peer/seeder stats off the same client -- replacing the old pattern of spinning up a scratch client just to fetch a magnet's info dict and discarding it immediately.

This isn't just an efficiency choice: the doc comment on PreviewSession spells out why the real download afterward still needs its own fresh torrent.Client rather than reusing this one. tocli's process-per-torrent design requires the foreground interactive process to be able to exit immediately once the user confirms, and the actual download runs in a separately spawned background process (via SpawnAndTrack/process.SpawnDetached). Handing PreviewSession's live client across that process boundary isn't achievable in Go: a raw fork() without an immediate exec() is unsafe in a multi-threaded Go binary, and exec() replaces the process image entirely, so it can't transplant an already-open client's in-memory state or live socket connections into the new process either way. So PreviewSession only eliminates redundant work *within* the single foreground preview (one client instead of two); the spawned child's own fresh torrent.Client and fresh peer discovery are an unavoidable cost of the process boundary, not something this change was trying to remove.

Bundled in the same commit: RemoveTorrent's withData path previously treated any os.Stat failure on tc.SavePath as a hard failure. Now it checks os.IsNotExist specifically and sets the new RemoveOutcome.DataAlreadyGone (not an error) when the save path is simply gone -- e.g. the user already moved or deleted it by hand -- while a real stat/removal failure still populates RemoveOutcome.DataRemoveErr. Either way the tracking entry itself is still removed, so a missing save path can no longer leave a remove stuck failing outright, and a genuine removal failure still doesn't orphan the tracking entry.
